# M3_limitation_linear_HH.ode -- the LIMITATION of method M3 (boundary value continuation),
#              reduced Hodgkin-Huxley neuron (Osinga & Moehlis 2010): the end point must stay on the STRAIGHT piece of the isochron
#
# ======================================================================================
# WHAT TO DO IN THE XPPAUT WINDOW   (or run M3_limitation_linear_HH.xps: File > Do script)
# ======================================================================================
#   The same steps as M3_HH.ode, with k = 1 (segments one period long) instead of 3:
#   1. Initialconds > Go (keys i g): Γ from gamma0, one period.   2. File > Auto (keys f a).
#   3. AUTO Numerics: Ntst 100  Nmax 900  Ds -0.01  Dsmin 1e-8  Dsmax 0.5  Par Min -100
#      Par Max 55  Norm Max 1e6  ITNW 8  SuppBP 1  (Ds -0.01: to the left first).
#   4. Run > Bdry Value (r b): to the left.   5. As step 5 of M3_HH.ode: back to the
#      start values, Go, Numerics Ds 0.01 Nmax 60, Run > Bdry Value: to the right.
#
# ======================================================================================
# WHAT GOES WRONG, AND WHY
# ======================================================================================
#  M3 asks each segment to end on the STRAIGHT piece gamma0 + dl e of the isochron.  That
#  straight piece is the isochron only near Γ (|dl| small; here |dl| below about 0.001).
#  With one period, points far along the isochron cannot reach that small piece in time:
#  past the equilibrium the continuation has to push dl far out, the end point leaves the
#  isochron, and from there on the start points u(0) are NOT on the isochron of phase 0:
#  the curve runs off to (-100, 0.51), a branch of points whose phase is about 0.42, not 0.
#  Watch the column dl in the AUTO output (the terminal lines / the point's data):
#  small = good point, large = wrong point.
#  Checked (pyauto-xpp): the points with |dl| < 0.001 lie on the paper's isochron;
#  the run-off branch (|dl| about 110) has phase about 0.42.
#  The cure (Osinga & Moehlis): longer segments, k = 2, 3, ... periods: the end point then
#  needs only a tiny dl.  That is M3_HH.ode (k = 3).
# ======================================================================================

# --- the continuation parameter p = v(0) (first, so that it is AUTO's Par1)
par p=44.706379

# --- the model: the reduced Hodgkin-Huxley neuron of Osinga & Moehlis (2010), eq. (4.1):
#     m = minf(v) (fast), h = 0.8 - n;  I = 10 (repetitive firing).  Here w is n.
am(x)=0.1*(x+40)/(1-exp(-(x+40)/10))
bm(x)=4*exp(-(x+65)/18)
an(x)=0.01*(x+55)/(1-exp(-(x+55)/10))
bn(x)=0.125*exp(-(x+65)/80)
minf(x)=am(x)/(am(x)+bm(x))
fx(x,y)=iapp-gna*minf(x)^3*(0.8-y)*(x-ena)-gk*y^4*(x-ek)-gl*(x-el)
fy(x,y)=an(x)*(1-y)-bn(x)*y
par iapp=10,gna=120,gk=36,gl=0.3,ena=50,ek=-77,el=-54.4

# --- the ingredients
par per=11.84628522, k=1
par gv0=44.706379, gw0=0.45973791, ev=0.9999999906, ew=-0.0001371078909

# --- step 1: the segment in rescaled time, u' = kT F(u)
v'=k*per*fx(v,w)
w'=k*per*fy(v,w)
# --- dl and the copy w0 of w(0): constants along the segment
dl'=0
w0'=0
init v=44.706379,w=0.45973791,dl=0,w0=0.45973791

# --- step 2: the boundary conditions (v' here means v at s = 1, v means v at s = 0)
bndry v'-gv0-dl'*ev
bndry w'-gw0-dl'*ew
bndry v-p
bndry w0-w

@ meth=rk4, dt=0.0001, total=1, maxstor=200000, bound=1e6
@ xp=v, yp=w, xlo=-100, xhi=55, ylo=0.3, yhi=0.8
@ ntst=100, ncol=4, nmax=900, npr=100000, ds=-0.01, dsmin=1e-8, dsmax=0.5, parmin=-100, parmax=55
@ normmax=1e6, epsl=1e-8, epsu=1e-8, epss=1e-6
# nullcline colours as in the paper: V-nullcline green (7), n-nullcline red (1)
@ xnc=7, ync=1
@ autovar=w0, autoxmin=-100, autoxmax=55, autoymin=0.3, autoymax=0.8
done
